## Creating a powerup from an existing repo

You can also create a powerup from an existing repo's code instead of starting
starting from scratch:

```sh
pup create saas-starter \ 
  --capture=all \ 
  --description="Scaffolds a SaaS starter" \
  --intent="Saas, Nextjs with tailwind and auth0" \
  --variables=theme,projectName,auth0ClientId,auth0Domain,auth0Audience \
  --type=single-use
```

`--capture=all` turns the repo's new and modified files into the powerup's
templates;

`--capture=workingDir` limits the capture to your the current uncommitted git changes.

## The index.ts file

The captured files are raw copies — hand the parameterization to your AI
agent:

- convert the captured code into `.ts` templates that receive the powerup's
  variables and return the full file contents as a string,
- declare those variables in the powerup's `index.ts`
  (`variables.required` / `variables.optional`),
- add or adjust `steps` (`create`, `modify`, `delete`, `read`,
  `install`).

A template is a small function from variables to file contents:

```ts
export default ({ componentName, theme }: Record<string, string>) =>
  `<button class="${theme}">${componentName}</button>`;
```

